@file ComparatorChallenges/LiebThirring.lean
@decl OAI.SharpLiebThirring.sharp_lieb_thirring
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Sharp finite-matrix Lieb–Thirring inequalities and all equality cases
-- L4DEL: intuition: The formalized result determines the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring constant for $1/2<\gamma<3/2$. For every nonnegative $W\in L^{\gamma+1/2}(\mathbb R)$, it bounds the full negative-eigenvalue moment of $-d^2/dx^2-W$ by the one-bound-state constant times the potential integral. This constant is optimal and is attained by $(r+1)\mathrm{sech}^2(rx)$, where $r=(\gamma-1/2)^{-1}$. No finite spectral cutoff is imposed.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Analysis.LiebThirring.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/LipschitzEquivalence.lean
@decl OAI.LipschitzCounterexample.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Lipschitz equivalent Banach spaces need not be linearly isomorphic
-- L4DEL: intuition: The formalized counterexample gives separable real Banach spaces $X,Y$ that are bi-Lipschitz equivalent but not linearly isomorphic. The bijection has lower Lipschitz bound $4/21$ and upper bound $76/25$. The linear obstruction is explicit: $X$ contains a linear isometric copy of $c_0(\ell_2)$, whereas $Y$ contains no bounded linear copy of that space. The formalized result constructs one separable real Banach space $X$ that is bi-Lipschitz equivalent to $X\times c_0$ but contains no closed linear subspace isomorphic to $c_0$. The same space contains a bi-Lipschitz copy of $c_0$, is bi-Lipschitz universal for separable metric spaces, and is not linearly isomorphic to $X\times c_0$. Thus nonlinear absorption of $c_0$ does not force a linear copy of it.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Analysis.LipschitzEquivalence.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/LipschitzHilbert.lean
@decl OAI.LipschitzHilbert.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Hilbert transforms along Lipschitz directions
-- L4DEL: intuition: The formalization proves uniform $L^2$ bounds for short Hilbert transforms along Lipschitz unit vector fields in the plane, including fields depending on both coordinates. For Lipschitz constant $K>0$, every hard truncation with $0<\varepsilon\le R\le1/(10^6K)$ has one universal $L^2$ bound on Schwartz functions, independent of the inner cutoff. At a fixed short scale for $1$-Lipschitz fields, the formalization also gives principal-value and weak-$(2,2)$ estimates and bounded extensions to all of $L^2$. These results establish the stated short-scale form of Stein's question.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Analysis.LipschitzHilbert.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

